<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Paul IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1476">1476</a>–<a href="/years/1559">1559</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Gian Pietro Carafa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul IV, born Gian Pietro Carafa, was pope from 1555 to 1559 and a leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>. A co-founder of the Theatine order, he strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-inquisition">Roman Inquisition</a> and issued the first papal Index of Forbidden Books in 1559. His bull Cum nimis absurdum of 1555 confined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to a walled ghetto, and his war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ended in defeat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iv.jpg" alt="Pope Paul IV" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manner of Jacopino del Conte · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pope_Paul_IV_%E2%80%93_Jacopino_Conte_(Manner),_ca._1560.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
